{"qid": "683a58c9a7e7fe4e7695848b", "artifact": "data/outputs/aus_agent_v2/20260806T220524596862+1000.i_m_currently_working_on.output.json", "system": "You are the final plan compiler for a research system with a hard 1,024-word\nanswer limit. You receive the original request, a primary coverage plan, and an\nindependent scout inventory. Produce one compact execution contract for the\nresearcher; do not answer the request or state unsupported facts.\n\nThe primary plan and scout are proposals, not cumulative obligations. Resolve\noverlap and priority instead of concatenating them. Preserve, in this order:\n1. every explicit user component, deliverable form, audience constraint, and\n   repeated per-part requirement;\n2. safety, privacy, legal, temporal, and scope constraints whose omission could\n   make the answer wrong or harmful;\n3. the exact evidence types needed for central claims, including a concrete\n   prior measurement when the request proposes a study or comparison;\n4. at most three distinct latent expectations with the highest coverage gained\n   per answer word.\n\nDrop optional breadth, duplicate formulations, exhaustive taxonomies, source\nshopping, and deeper variants of an already-covered concept. Never let a\nlatent addition displace an explicit requirement, a novice definition, or a\nlegal/safety requirement. For a repeated tutorial, fund the requested elements\ninside each selected part before adding more branches. For a board report,\nretain execution ownership and economics without crowding out the requested\ncountry comparisons. For a definite proof, retain both the conclusion and the\nfew canonical named mechanisms needed to explain it.\n\nReturn 8 to 12 numbered items under 475 words. Each item is one sentence of at\nmost 45 words and starts with one label: DELIVERABLE, AUDIENCE, CORE, SAFETY,\nEVIDENCE, LATENT, or BUDGET. Use no more than three LATENT items, each covering\none coherent expectation rather than a packed wishlist. The final BUDGET item\nmust allocate at most 950 answer words across the deliverable. Return only the\ncompiled plan.\n", "request": "ORIGINAL RESEARCH REQUEST\n\nI'm currently working on a project. The professor wasn't clear, she just said that I will use NLP techniques on some data, de-identified Electronic Health Records, to extract Asian subgroups, and try to abstract the same subgroups as are in CDC WONDER data. Then, I'll apply this to look at prevalence rates of autoimmune diseases. I have no clue what she means. Can you first help explain what she might mean? I don't know examples of what Electronic Health Records look like. What NLP techniques might I be using on data like this? What subgroups in CDC WONDER data would I be abstracting? How could I apply this to look at prevalence rates of autoimmune diseases? What skills should I brush up on? Please help me create a plan for my research going forward, according to what the professor said.\n\nPRIMARY PLAN\n\n1. **DELIVERABLE:** Produce one concise briefing in six parts: (1) likely interpretation of the assignment, (2) illustrative de-identified EHR examples, (3) CDC WONDER Asian subgroup mapping, (4) NLP pipeline, (5) autoimmune-disease prevalence design, and (6) staged skills/research plan with questions for the professor.\n\n2. **AUDIENCE:** Write for a student with basic programming or statistics but no assumed knowledge of clinical NLP, EHR data models, epidemiology, or CDC WONDER; progress from a plain-language project summary to an implementable pilot protocol.\n\n3. **EXPLICIT:** Explain what “extract/abstract Asian subgroups” could mean; show at least two synthetic EHR record/text examples; describe applicable NLP techniques; identify relevant WONDER subgroup categories; explain prevalence estimation; and provide skills to review plus concrete next steps.\n\n4. **DEFINITION:** Define EHR, de-identification, structured versus unstructured fields, NLP, named-entity recognition, text classification, ontology/code mapping, race versus ethnicity/ancestry/national origin, case definition, numerator, denominator, prevalence, confidence interval, validation, precision, recall, and F1 score.\n\n5. **IMPLIED:** Distinguish extracting subgroup identity from clinical text versus harmonizing existing race/ethnicity fields, and distinguish CDC WONDER mortality categories from a valid prevalence dataset; cover ICD-9/10-CM, SNOMED CT, medications/labs, missingness, “Asian, not otherwise specified,” multiracial identity, temporal scope, and denominator eligibility.\n\n6. **EXAMPLE:** Include a worked pipeline on at least two synthetic cases—such as explicit self-identification versus indirect or ambiguous evidence—showing source text, annotation, model output, subgroup mapping, uncertainty handling, disease ascertainment, and why surname, language, birthplace, or clinician inference must not be treated uncritically as identity.\n\n7. **EVIDENCE:** Verify current subgroup labels, years, geography, suppression rules, and race-bridging practices directly in the relevant CDC WONDER dataset documentation; search Census/OMB race standards and the local EHR’s data dictionary because available categories may differ by dataset, year, and institution.\n\n8. **EVIDENCE:** Find at least one concrete prior EHR/NLP study that identifies detailed Asian race/ethnicity and one validated EHR autoimmune phenotyping or prevalence study; extract sample size, reference standard, performance, case definition, denominator, and reported limitations rather than extrapolating unsupported prevalence figures.\n\n9. **IMPLIED:** Compare rule-based dictionaries/regular expressions, classical supervised classifiers, transformer-based clinical language models, and hybrid structured-plus-text methods; address annotation guidelines, dual review and adjudication, train/validation/test separation, class imbalance, negation, family-history/context errors, domain shift, and subgroup-specific error analysis.\n\n10. **SAFETY:** Require IRB/data-governance review, minimum-necessary access, secure computing, disclosure controls, and consultation with affected communities; flag re-identification, stereotyping, biased proxies, small-cell reporting, and the ethical difference between self-reported identity and inferred identity.\n\n11. **BUDGET:** Allocate at most 930 words: interpretation 110; EHR examples 130; WONDER mapping 130; NLP pipeline 180; prevalence design 190; skills and phased plan 150; professor clarification checklist and takeaways 40.\n\nBLIND SCOUT INVENTORY\n\n[{\"requirement\": \"Clarify the intended target before modeling: identify the specific CDC WONDER database, years, geography, Asian subgroup variables/coding scheme, EHR fields available, and whether the task is subgroup extraction from structured fields, free text, or both.\", \"reason\": \"CDC WONDER contains multiple datasets and schemas, so neither the target labels nor the meaning of matching or abstracting subgroups is well-defined without this scope check.\", \"search_leads\": [\"CDC WONDER dataset Asian subgroup race categories data dictionary\", \"CDC WONDER bridged-race multiple cause mortality race coding\", \"EHR Asian ethnicity subgroup NLP cohort identification\"]}, {\"requirement\": \"Include a concrete end-to-end workflow with a small synthetic EHR example: inspect notes and structured demographics, create annotation guidelines and a labeled gold standard, normalize mentions to CDC-compatible labels, split data at the patient level, train baselines and NLP models, perform error analysis, and validate before prevalence estimation.\", \"reason\": \"A novice needs to see what the input, output, labels, and research stages actually look like; a list of algorithms alone will not make the assignment actionable.\", \"search_leads\": [\"clinical NLP ethnicity extraction annotation guidelines\", \"medspaCy rule based clinical NLP tutorial\", \"patient-level train test split clinical NLP validation\"]}, {\"requirement\": \"Cover alternative extraction methods and evaluation: structured-code lookup, keyword/regular-expression rules, dictionary or ontology matching, classical TF-IDF classifiers, clinical transformer or named-entity-recognition models, and human review; compare subgroup-level precision, recall, F1, confusion matrices, and inter-annotator agreement.\", \"reason\": \"Rare subgroups, spelling variants, contextual mentions, and class imbalance make overall accuracy misleading and require both simple baselines and robust validation.\", \"search_leads\": [\"clinical NLP race ethnicity extraction named entity recognition\", \"imbalanced multiclass clinical NLP macro F1\", \"Cohen kappa clinical annotation inter annotator agreement\"]}, {\"requirement\": \"Define the epidemiologic analysis precisely: operationalize each autoimmune disease with validated phenotype algorithms, distinguish prevalence from incidence and mortality, specify numerator, denominator, index date and observation window, handle repeated records and missing subgroup data, and plan age/sex standardization, confidence intervals, and sensitivity analyses.\", \"reason\": \"Extracted subgroup labels do not by themselves yield valid prevalence rates; cohort construction, disease ascertainment, denominators, and comparability with CDC data are separate methodological problems.\", \"search_leads\": [\"electronic health record autoimmune disease computable phenotype validation\", \"EHR prevalence numerator denominator observation window\", \"direct age standardization prevalence confidence interval race ethnicity\"]}, {\"requirement\": \"Address governance and validity risks: de-identification limits, IRB and HIPAA/data-use requirements, secure computing, minimum-cell suppression, self-identification versus inferred ethnicity, subgroup aggregation and mapping loss, selection and documentation bias, missing-not-at-random data, and fairness audits with stakeholder review.\", \"reason\": \"Inferring granular ethnicity from clinical records is sensitive, and privacy constraints plus systematic EHR and label biases can invalidate subgroup comparisons or create harms even when model metrics appear strong.\", \"search_leads\": [\"HIPAA de-identified EHR NLP IRB data use agreement\", \"race ethnicity inference EHR bias missingness fairness\", \"small cell suppression health data Asian subgroup reporting\"]}]", "raw": "1. DELIVERABLE: Produce a six-part briefing covering assignment interpretation, synthetic EHR examples, CDC WONDER subgroup mapping, an NLP workflow, autoimmune-disease prevalence design, and a phased learning/research plan ending with clarification questions for the professor.\n\n2. AUDIENCE: Write for a novice in clinical NLP and epidemiology, defining on first use EHR, de-identification, structured and unstructured data, NLP, classification, named-entity recognition, ontology mapping, case definition, prevalence, confidence interval, precision, recall, and F1.\n\n3. CORE: Explain that “abstract the same subgroups” may mean extracting self-reported Asian identities from structured fields, clinical text, or both, then harmonizing them to a specified WONDER coding scheme rather than inferring identity from unreliable proxies.\n\n4. CORE: Walk through at least two synthetic de-identified records—one explicit and one ambiguous—showing demographics, note text, annotation, contextual or negation handling, subgroup output, CDC-compatible mapping, uncertainty, autoimmune phenotype assignment, and exclusion or human-review decisions.\n\n5. EVIDENCE: Verify in official documentation the exact WONDER database, subgroup labels, coding scheme, years, geography, suppression and race-bridging rules, while checking current OMB or Census standards and the institution’s EHR data dictionary for incompatible categories.\n\n6. CORE: Compare structured-field lookup, dictionaries and regular expressions, classical classifiers, clinical transformers or named-entity recognition, and hybrid methods; specify annotation guidelines, dual review and adjudication, patient-level splits, class-imbalance handling, confusion matrices, subgroup metrics, and error analysis.\n\n7. CORE: Explain prevalence using a validated disease case definition, unique-patient numerator, eligible-population denominator, index date and observation window; address repeated encounters, missing subgroup data, age/sex adjustment, confidence intervals, sensitivity analyses, and differences among prevalence, incidence, and mortality.\n\n8. EVIDENCE: Locate one detailed-Asian-identity EHR/NLP study and one validated EHR autoimmune phenotyping or prevalence study, reporting each study’s sample size, population, reference standard, concrete performance or prevalence measurement, case definition, denominator, and limitations.\n\n9. SAFETY: Require IRB and data-governance confirmation, minimum-necessary access, secure computing, disclosure controls, and small-cell safeguards; distinguish self-identification from inference and discuss re-identification, proxy stereotyping, mapping loss, selection bias, documentation bias, fairness, and community review.\n\n10. CORE: Provide a staged plan covering SQL or data wrangling, Python, regular expressions, clinical terminologies, annotation, NLP modeling, evaluation, and epidemiology, with milestones for scoping, pilot labeling, baselines, validation, prevalence analysis, reporting, and professor decisions.\n\n11. BUDGET: Allocate at most 930 words: interpretation 100, definitions and EHR examples 125, WONDER mapping 115, NLP workflow 170, prevalence design 185, safety and validity 85, skills and phased plan 110, and professor clarification checklist 40.", "normalized": "1. DELIVERABLE: Produce a six-part briefing covering assignment interpretation, synthetic EHR examples, CDC WONDER subgroup mapping, an NLP workflow, autoimmune-disease prevalence design, and a phased learning/research plan ending with clarification questions for the professor.\n\n2. AUDIENCE: Write for a novice in clinical NLP and epidemiology, defining on first use EHR, de-identification, structured and unstructured data, NLP, classification, named-entity recognition, ontology mapping, case definition, prevalence, confidence interval, precision, recall, and F1.\n\n3. CORE: Explain that “abstract the same subgroups” may mean extracting self-reported Asian identities from structured fields, clinical text, or both, then harmonizing them to a specified WONDER coding scheme rather than inferring identity from unreliable proxies.\n\n4. CORE: Walk through at least two synthetic de-identified records—one explicit and one ambiguous—showing demographics, note text, annotation, contextual or negation handling, subgroup output, CDC-compatible mapping, uncertainty, autoimmune phenotype assignment, and exclusion or human-review decisions.\n\n5. EVIDENCE: Verify in official documentation the exact WONDER database, subgroup labels, coding scheme, years, geography, suppression and race-bridging rules, while checking current OMB or Census standards and the institution’s EHR data dictionary for incompatible categories.\n\n6. CORE: Compare structured-field lookup, dictionaries and regular expressions, classical classifiers, clinical transformers or named-entity recognition, and hybrid methods; specify annotation guidelines, dual review and adjudication, patient-level splits, class-imbalance handling, confusion matrices, subgroup metrics, and error analysis.\n\n7. CORE: Explain prevalence using a validated disease case definition, unique-patient numerator, eligible-population denominator, index date and observation window; address repeated encounters, missing subgroup data, age/sex adjustment, confidence intervals, sensitivity analyses, and differences among prevalence, incidence, and mortality.\n\n8. EVIDENCE: Locate one detailed-Asian-identity EHR/NLP study and one validated EHR autoimmune phenotyping or prevalence study, reporting each study’s sample size, population, reference standard, concrete performance or prevalence measurement, case definition, denominator, and limitations.\n\n9. SAFETY: Require IRB and data-governance confirmation, minimum-necessary access, secure computing, disclosure controls, and small-cell safeguards; distinguish self-identification from inference and discuss re-identification, proxy stereotyping, mapping loss, selection bias, documentation bias, fairness, and community review.\n\n10. CORE: Provide a staged plan covering SQL or data wrangling, Python, regular expressions, clinical terminologies, annotation, NLP modeling, evaluation, and epidemiology, with milestones for scoping, pilot labeling, baselines, validation, prevalence analysis, reporting, and professor decisions.\n\n11. BUDGET: Allocate at most 930 words: interpretation 100, definitions and EHR examples 125, WONDER mapping 115, NLP workflow 170, prevalence design 185, safety and validity 85, skills and phased plan 110, and professor clarification checklist 40.", "usage": {"input_tokens": 1984, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0, "output_tokens": 1094}}
{"qid": "6847465956a0f6376a605391", "artifact": "data/outputs/aus_agent_v2/20260806T220524619361+1000.write_a_strategic_business_report.output.json", "system": "You are the final plan compiler for a research system with a hard 1,024-word\nanswer limit. You receive the original request, a primary coverage plan, and an\nindependent scout inventory. Produce one compact execution contract for the\nresearcher; do not answer the request or state unsupported facts.\n\nThe primary plan and scout are proposals, not cumulative obligations. Resolve\noverlap and priority instead of concatenating them. Preserve, in this order:\n1. every explicit user component, deliverable form, audience constraint, and\n   repeated per-part requirement;\n2. safety, privacy, legal, temporal, and scope constraints whose omission could\n   make the answer wrong or harmful;\n3. the exact evidence types needed for central claims, including a concrete\n   prior measurement when the request proposes a study or comparison;\n4. at most three distinct latent expectations with the highest coverage gained\n   per answer word.\n\nDrop optional breadth, duplicate formulations, exhaustive taxonomies, source\nshopping, and deeper variants of an already-covered concept. Never let a\nlatent addition displace an explicit requirement, a novice definition, or a\nlegal/safety requirement. For a repeated tutorial, fund the requested elements\ninside each selected part before adding more branches. For a board report,\nretain execution ownership and economics without crowding out the requested\ncountry comparisons. For a definite proof, retain both the conclusion and the\nfew canonical named mechanisms needed to explain it.\n\nReturn 8 to 12 numbered items under 475 words. Each item is one sentence of at\nmost 45 words and starts with one label: DELIVERABLE, AUDIENCE, CORE, SAFETY,\nEVIDENCE, LATENT, or BUDGET. Use no more than three LATENT items, each covering\none coherent expectation rather than a packed wishlist. The final BUDGET item\nmust allocate at most 950 answer words across the deliverable. Return only the\ncompiled plan.\n", "request": "ORIGINAL RESEARCH REQUEST\n\nWrite a strategic business report advising a mid-stage U.S. plant-based meat startup on how to enter and scale in the Southeast Asian market (focusing on countries like Singapore, Thailand, and Indonesia). Your report should address: 1. A comparative analysis of consumer demand trends for alternative proteins in each target country. 2. Competitive landscape: key regional players, pricing, retail/distribution channels, and regulatory hurdles. 3. Cultural and dietary considerations that may influence adoption and positioning. 4. Go-to-market recommendations such as direct-to-consumer, B2B partnerships, and quick service restaurants. 5. Risks and opportunities associated with regulatory shifts, supply chain localization, and sustainability branding. Use regional market data, public reports, consumer surveys, and industry benchmarks. Assume your audience is a board of venture capital-backed startup executives seeking a data-driven, execution-ready plan.\n\nPRIMARY PLAN\n\n1. **DELIVERABLE:** Produce one data-driven, execution-ready strategic business report covering three target countries—Singapore, Thailand, and Indonesia—with an executive recommendation, comparative country analysis, market-entry strategy, risks, and phased implementation roadmap.\n\n2. **AUDIENCE:** Write for venture capital-backed startup executives familiar with unit economics, fundraising, and go-to-market strategy but not necessarily Southeast Asian food regulation; prioritize decisions, quantified trade-offs, ownership, timing, and measurable milestones.\n\n3. **EXPLICIT:** For every country, assess alternative-protein demand trends, at least two relevant competitors, pricing, retail and foodservice distribution, regulatory hurdles, cultural/dietary adoption factors, and the viability of DTC, B2B, and QSR partnerships. Compare regulatory shifts, localized supply chains, and sustainability branding as both opportunities and risks.\n\n4. **IMPLIED:** Include market-entry sequencing, target customer segments, product/format adaptation, premium versus price-parity positioning, importer/distributor versus local manufacturing or co-packing, halal strategy, cold-chain needs, partner-selection criteria, likely margins, and defensibility. Address incumbent animal protein, tofu/tempeh, and other familiar meat alternatives as substitutes—not only branded plant-based meats.\n\n5. **DEFINITION:** Briefly define alternative protein, plant-based meat, TAM/SAM/SOM, DTC, B2B, QSR, price parity, co-packing, and halal certification; distinguish market size forecasts from observed sales, stated consumer intent from repeat purchase, and retail shelf price from foodservice economics.\n\n6. **EVIDENCE:** Search current country-level sales and growth data from Euromonitor, Statista/GlobalData where accessible, GFI APAC, OECD-FAO, government statistics, and major retailer or delivery-platform observations. Find at least two public reports or consumer-survey sources per country, recording field dates, sample sizes, methodology, currency, and whether figures cover all alternative proteins or plant-based meat specifically.\n\n7. **EVIDENCE:** Build a competitor/pricing table from current company sites, FairPrice/RedMart, Lotus’s/Big C/Tops, and Tokopedia/Indomaret/major Indonesian grocers, supplemented by restaurant menus and delivery apps. Normalize package prices into USD and local currency per 100 grams or serving, while flagging promotions, taxes, and non-comparable formats.\n\n8. **EVIDENCE:** Verify rules through Singapore Food Agency and MUIS, Thailand’s FDA and halal authorities, and Indonesia’s BPOM, Ministry of Trade, and BPJPH, including import registration, labeling, novel-food status, claims, tariffs, and halal deadlines. Treat all regulatory details as time- and jurisdiction-sensitive, citing effective dates and avoiding assumptions based on secondary summaries.\n\n9. **EXAMPLE:** Develop one concise entry case per country: Singapore as a premium launch/test hub, Thailand as a foodservice/manufacturing partnership case, and Indonesia as a scale-and-halal localization case. Each should show target segment, adapted product, channel partner type, indicative pricing logic, key obstacle, and 12–24-month success metric without presupposing the conclusion.\n\n10. **IMPLIED:** Evaluate demand uncertainty, weak repeat purchase, price sensitivity, taste/texture mismatch, regulatory or halal delays, distributor dependence, cold-chain and ingredient exposure, currency risk, greenwashing scrutiny, incumbent response, and cannibalization. Balance bullish forecasts with counterevidence such as category slowdowns, brand exits, discounting, or survey–purchase gaps.\n\n11. **DELIVERABLE:** End with a prioritized country sequence, go/no-go gates, 0–6/6–12/12–24-month actions, partnership shortlist criteria, and KPIs covering trial, repeat rate, velocity, gross margin, customer acquisition cost, foodservice reorder rate, localization, and regulatory progress.\n\nBLIND SCOUT INVENTORY\n\n[{\"requirement\": \"Produce a weighted country-prioritization and phased 24-month entry roadmap with launch sequence, accountable executives, KPIs, decision gates, pilot budgets, and stop/scale criteria.\", \"reason\": \"A board needs an executable capital-allocation plan, not three parallel market descriptions; sequencing should connect market attractiveness, regulatory readiness, channel access, and organizational capacity.\", \"search_leads\": [\"Singapore Thailand Indonesia alternative protein market entry benchmarks\", \"Southeast Asia plant based meat sales channel market size 2024\", \"alternative protein startup international expansion KPI pilot framework\"]}, {\"requirement\": \"Model country- and channel-specific unit economics, including target retail/menu prices, willingness-to-pay and meat-price benchmarks, landed COGS, tariffs, cold-chain costs, distributor and retailer margins, promotional spend, and sensitivity cases for import versus local production.\", \"reason\": \"Demand and competitor pricing do not establish commercial viability; executives need gross-margin, cash-burn, break-even-volume, and capital gates before choosing DTC, retail, QSR, co-manufacturing, or owned production.\", \"search_leads\": [\"plant based meat Southeast Asia price premium consumer willingness to pay survey\", \"Singapore Thailand Indonesia food distributor retail margin cold chain costs\", \"alternative protein import tariff HS code Singapore Thailand Indonesia\"]}, {\"requirement\": \"Build a country-by-country market-access matrix naming responsible agencies and detailing novel-food or ingredient review, import registration, labeling and nutrition rules, environmental-claim standards, halal pathways, timelines, costs, and relevant government incentive programs.\", \"reason\": \"Generic references to regulation conceal launch-critical dependencies, particularly certification, religious compliance, claims substantiation, and approval lead times that can alter product formulation and entry sequencing.\", \"search_leads\": [\"Singapore SFA alternative protein novel food labeling grants\", \"Thailand FDA plant based meat import labeling BOI incentives\", \"Indonesia BPOM halal certification plant based food import rules\"]}, {\"requirement\": \"Translate cultural analysis into product and positioning choices by segment and eating occasion: preferred formats, local dishes, taste and texture expectations, spice profiles, pack sizes, cooking methods, religious constraints, health perceptions, and sustainability-message testing.\", \"reason\": \"Country-level demand totals are insufficient for product-market fit; adoption depends on whether the offering works in familiar meals and whether benefits resonate with flexitarians, families, foodservice buyers, and other priority segments.\", \"search_leads\": [\"Singapore Thailand Indonesia alternative protein consumer survey taste price health\", \"Southeast Asia plant based meat local cuisine product format study\", \"Indonesia Thailand Singapore flexitarian segmentation foodservice survey\"]}, {\"requirement\": \"Recommend a defensible operating model and partner pipeline, comparing import-distribution, licensing, joint venture, local co-manufacturing, and owned capacity; identify partner-selection criteria, supply resilience measures, strategic moats, financing needs, and long-term options.\", \"reason\": \"Channel recommendations alone do not explain who will manufacture, distribute, certify, sell, and fund the business or how the startup will preserve differentiation against regional incumbents while localizing supply.\", \"search_leads\": [\"Southeast Asia plant based meat co manufacturer distributor QSR partnership\", \"alternative protein Singapore Thailand Indonesia regional players funding capacity\", \"food startup Southeast Asia joint venture licensing localization strategy\"]}]", "raw": "1. DELIVERABLE: Produce a citation-rich strategic report comparing Singapore, Thailand, and Indonesia, ending with a prioritized entry sequence, channel strategy, investment recommendation, and phased 24-month execution roadmap.\n\n2. AUDIENCE: Write for venture-backed startup executives, emphasizing decisions, quantified trade-offs, capital efficiency, ownership, and milestones while briefly defining unfamiliar regulatory, halal, channel, and unit-economic terms.\n\n3. CORE: For each country, assess observed demand and growth, consumer segments, competitors, normalized pricing, retail and foodservice channels, regulatory barriers, cultural and dietary adoption factors, and DTC, B2B, and QSR viability.\n\n4. CORE: Translate local cuisines, eating occasions, religious requirements, taste expectations, health perceptions, sustainability attitudes, and price sensitivity into recommended product formats, formulations, pack sizes, positioning, and partner types.\n\n5. CORE: Compare risks and opportunities from regulatory change, halal compliance, import dependence, cold-chain constraints, ingredient sourcing, currency exposure, local co-packing or manufacturing, sustainability claims, distributor dependence, incumbent responses, and weak repeat purchasing.\n\n6. SAFETY: Verify import, ingredient or novel-food approval, labeling, claims, tariffs, and halal requirements through current official authorities, state effective dates and jurisdictional uncertainty, and avoid treating secondary summaries or pending rules as law.\n\n7. EVIDENCE: Support each country with at least one dated observed market measurement, one public consumer survey reporting sample and method, and relevant government, industry, or public-report benchmarks, clearly separating sales from forecasts and stated intent from repeat purchase.\n\n8. EVIDENCE: Build a current competitor matrix with at least two relevant players per country, product and channel presence, local and USD prices normalized per 100 grams or serving, conventional-protein comparisons, promotion flags, and dated retailer, platform, menu, or company evidence.\n\n9. LATENT: Model channel-specific economics for import versus localization, including landed COGS, intermediary margins, target shelf or menu price, gross margin, promotional burden, break-even logic, and sensitivity to volume, exchange rates, tariffs, and cold-chain costs.\n\n10. LATENT: Assign accountable executive owners, pilot budgets, partner-selection criteria, KPIs, and go/no-go or stop/scale gates across 0–6, 6–12, and 12–24 months, including trial, repeat, velocity, reorder rate, CAC, margin, localization, and regulatory progress.\n\n11. BUDGET: Allocate at most 900 words: 90 executive recommendation, 390 country comparison, 170 channel and unit economics, 150 roadmap and ownership, and 100 risks, evidence caveats, and decision gates.", "normalized": "1. DELIVERABLE: Produce a citation-rich strategic report comparing Singapore, Thailand, and Indonesia, ending with a prioritized entry sequence, channel strategy, investment recommendation, and phased 24-month execution roadmap.\n\n2. AUDIENCE: Write for venture-backed startup executives, emphasizing decisions, quantified trade-offs, capital efficiency, ownership, and milestones while briefly defining unfamiliar regulatory, halal, channel, and unit-economic terms.\n\n3. CORE: For each country, assess observed demand and growth, consumer segments, competitors, normalized pricing, retail and foodservice channels, regulatory barriers, cultural and dietary adoption factors, and DTC, B2B, and QSR viability.\n\n4. CORE: Translate local cuisines, eating occasions, religious requirements, taste expectations, health perceptions, sustainability attitudes, and price sensitivity into recommended product formats, formulations, pack sizes, positioning, and partner types.\n\n5. CORE: Compare risks and opportunities from regulatory change, halal compliance, import dependence, cold-chain constraints, ingredient sourcing, currency exposure, local co-packing or manufacturing, sustainability claims, distributor dependence, incumbent responses, and weak repeat purchasing.\n\n6. SAFETY: Verify import, ingredient or novel-food approval, labeling, claims, tariffs, and halal requirements through current official authorities, state effective dates and jurisdictional uncertainty, and avoid treating secondary summaries or pending rules as law.\n\n7. EVIDENCE: Support each country with at least one dated observed market measurement, one public consumer survey reporting sample and method, and relevant government, industry, or public-report benchmarks, clearly separating sales from forecasts and stated intent from repeat purchase.\n\n8. EVIDENCE: Build a current competitor matrix with at least two relevant players per country, product and channel presence, local and USD prices normalized per 100 grams or serving, conventional-protein comparisons, promotion flags, and dated retailer, platform, menu, or company evidence.\n\n9. LATENT: Model channel-specific economics for import versus localization, including landed COGS, intermediary margins, target shelf or menu price, gross margin, promotional burden, break-even logic, and sensitivity to volume, exchange rates, tariffs, and cold-chain costs.\n\n10. LATENT: Assign accountable executive owners, pilot budgets, partner-selection criteria, KPIs, and go/no-go or stop/scale gates across 0–6, 6–12, and 12–24 months, including trial, repeat, velocity, reorder rate, CAC, margin, localization, and regulatory progress.\n\n11. BUDGET: Allocate at most 900 words: 90 executive recommendation, 390 country comparison, 170 channel and unit economics, 150 roadmap and ownership, and 100 risks, evidence caveats, and decision gates.", "usage": {"input_tokens": 2061, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0, "output_tokens": 1070}}
{"qid": "6847465956a0f6376a6053fb", "artifact": "data/outputs/aus_agent_v2/20260806T220524611575+1000.conduct_an_analysis_to_determine.output.json", "system": "You are the final plan compiler for a research system with a hard 1,024-word\nanswer limit. You receive the original request, a primary coverage plan, and an\nindependent scout inventory. Produce one compact execution contract for the\nresearcher; do not answer the request or state unsupported facts.\n\nThe primary plan and scout are proposals, not cumulative obligations. Resolve\noverlap and priority instead of concatenating them. Preserve, in this order:\n1. every explicit user component, deliverable form, audience constraint, and\n   repeated per-part requirement;\n2. safety, privacy, legal, temporal, and scope constraints whose omission could\n   make the answer wrong or harmful;\n3. the exact evidence types needed for central claims, including a concrete\n   prior measurement when the request proposes a study or comparison;\n4. at most three distinct latent expectations with the highest coverage gained\n   per answer word.\n\nDrop optional breadth, duplicate formulations, exhaustive taxonomies, source\nshopping, and deeper variants of an already-covered concept. Never let a\nlatent addition displace an explicit requirement, a novice definition, or a\nlegal/safety requirement. For a repeated tutorial, fund the requested elements\ninside each selected part before adding more branches. For a board report,\nretain execution ownership and economics without crowding out the requested\ncountry comparisons. For a definite proof, retain both the conclusion and the\nfew canonical named mechanisms needed to explain it.\n\nReturn 8 to 12 numbered items under 475 words. Each item is one sentence of at\nmost 45 words and starts with one label: DELIVERABLE, AUDIENCE, CORE, SAFETY,\nEVIDENCE, LATENT, or BUDGET. Use no more than three LATENT items, each covering\none coherent expectation rather than a packed wishlist. The final BUDGET item\nmust allocate at most 950 answer words across the deliverable. Return only the\ncompiled plan.\n", "request": "ORIGINAL RESEARCH REQUEST\n\nConduct an analysis to determine whether social media's overall impact on society has been more beneficial or detrimental. Argument should be structured by examining social media's impact across key domains of society. For each domain you must evaluate both positive and negative effects, using evidence from academic studies, journalist reports, case studies. After analyzing each domain, construct a definitive judgment that weighs these impacts on each other.\n\nPRIMARY PLAN\n\n1. **DELIVERABLE:** Produce one evidence-led analytical essay in five parts: framing and method, three domain analyses, and a final cross-domain verdict. Each domain must present positive and negative effects, weigh them directly, and include at least two academic studies, two journalistic reports, and two case studies.\n\n2. **AUDIENCE:** Write for an informed general audience with no assumed expertise in social-science methods or platform governance. Briefly explain causal inference, correlation, algorithmic amplification, engagement-based ranking, and content moderation.\n\n3. **EXPLICIT:** Analyze three broad domains: (1) democracy, news, and public information; (2) mental health and social relationships; and (3) economic opportunity, community mobilization, and crisis response. End each section with a domain-specific judgment before delivering one definitive overall judgment.\n\n4. **DEFINITION:** Define “social media,” “society,” “beneficial,” and “detrimental,” specifying whether messaging apps, video platforms, and professional networks are included. Judge effects using reach, magnitude, severity, duration, reversibility, distribution across groups, and confidence in causality.\n\n5. **IMPLIED:** Distinguish platforms, active versus passive use, users versus nonusers, short- versus long-term effects, and harms to individuals versus institutions. Include expected mechanisms and failure modes such as network effects, polarization, misinformation, harassment, surveillance, addiction-like use, unequal access, moderation errors, and concentrated platform power.\n\n6. **EXAMPLE:** Potential contrasting cases to verify include civic mobilization versus violence or election manipulation; social support versus adolescent distress or harassment; and small-business/disaster coordination versus scams, labor exploitation, or health misinformation. Use cases illustratively rather than treating prominent anecdotes as representative.\n\n7. **EVIDENCE:** Search Google Scholar, PubMed, JSTOR, SSRN, and major reviews for experiments, longitudinal studies, natural experiments, meta-analyses, and platform-data studies; verify anchors such as Facebook deactivation research, college-platform rollout studies, misinformation-exposure studies, and randomized usage-reduction trials.\n\n8. **EVIDENCE:** Search Reuters, AP, BBC, Financial Times, and other reputable outlets for independently documented cases and investigations, then triangulate claims with court records, regulator reports, platform disclosures, WHO/OECD/UN data, or election-monitoring institutions. Collect at least six distinct evidence items per domain while avoiding duplicate reporting of one underlying study.\n\n9. **IMPLIED:** Compare evidence across adolescents and adults, countries and political systems, socioeconomic groups, genders, and marginalized populations. Explicitly address Western and platform-data biases, self-selection, measurement error, publication bias, rapidly changing products, and the inability of observational associations to establish causation.\n\n10. **SAFETY:** Do not generalize a global verdict from one country, age group, platform, or election; seek counterevidence for every major claim and label uncertainty. Recheck time- and jurisdiction-sensitive facts about regulation, platform policies, user counts, elections, and pending litigation.\n\n11. **BUDGET:** Allocate 100 words to framing/method, 220 words to each of three domains, and 140 words to synthesis and verdict, totaling 900 words. Use a concise hook, clear transitions showing progression from individual to institutional effects, reusable weighing criteria, section takeaways, and compact citations.\n\nBLIND SCOUT INVENTORY\n\n[{\"requirement\": \"Define social media, the geographic and historical scope, the comparison baseline, and an explicit weighting rubric for the final judgment based on reach, effect size, severity, duration, reversibility, distribution, and confidence in causality.\", \"reason\": \"Without fixed scope and commensurable criteria, the conclusion risks becoming an impressionistic tally of unlike benefits and harms rather than a defensible overall judgment.\", \"search_leads\": [\"framework weighing social media societal benefits harms\", \"social media welfare cost benefit assessment\", \"social media causal evidence systematic review\"]}, {\"requirement\": \"Use a domain structure broad enough to include mental and physical health; relationships and community; politics, democracy, and information; education and knowledge; economy and work; public safety; privacy and surveillance; and inequality or vulnerable populations.\", \"reason\": \"A shallow analysis often overweights mental health and politics while omitting major social institutions and distributional consequences that could change the overall verdict.\", \"search_leads\": [\"social media societal impacts domains review\", \"social media effects education economy privacy inequality\", \"social media public health public safety evidence\"]}, {\"requirement\": \"Explain the platform mechanisms producing outcomes—including recommendation and ranking, virality, network effects, targeted advertising, moderation, anonymity, social comparison, and collective-action affordances—and distinguish causal findings from correlations and self-reports.\", \"reason\": \"Listing outcomes without mechanisms or evidence quality cannot show whether social media caused them, under what conditions they arise, or which harms and benefits are amenable to intervention.\", \"search_leads\": [\"social media algorithms causal effects field experiment\", \"social media mechanisms virality ranking moderation review\", \"social media deactivation randomized trial wellbeing politics\"]}, {\"requirement\": \"Compare effects across platforms, age groups, socioeconomic groups, political systems, and regions, incorporating non-Western cases and contrasting regulatory approaches rather than generalizing from a single population or platform.\", \"reason\": \"Impacts are heterogeneous, and a society-wide verdict is not credible if evidence is dominated by Western adult users or ignores institutional context and concentrated harms.\", \"search_leads\": [\"Global South social media societal impact case studies\", \"cross country social media effects regulation comparison\", \"adolescents marginalized groups social media differential effects\"]}, {\"requirement\": \"Triangulate academic studies, investigative journalism, and concrete case studies within each domain, covering both short- and long-run effects and testing prominent cases against broader measurements rather than treating anecdotes as representative.\", \"reason\": \"The request explicitly requires multiple evidence types; disciplined triangulation is needed to reconcile controlled findings, population-level trends, and real-world institutional failures or benefits.\", \"search_leads\": [\"social media case studies Arab Spring Myanmar COVID MeToo\", \"social media longitudinal effects meta analysis\", \"investigative journalism platform harms internal research\"]}]", "raw": "1. DELIVERABLE Produce a citation-rich analytical essay organized into framing, three domain sections, and synthesis, with each domain evaluating evidenced benefits and harms, directly weighing them, and ending in a domain-specific judgment before one definitive overall verdict.\n\n2. AUDIENCE Write for an informed general audience without assumed social-science or platform-governance expertise, defining social media, society, benefit, harm, causal inference, correlation, algorithmic amplification, engagement ranking, and content moderation concisely.\n\n3. CORE Set a global, historically bounded scope through the research date, specify included platform types, and compare observed outcomes with plausible pre-social-media or alternative-technology baselines rather than an unattainable harm-free society.\n\n4. CORE Examine democracy, news, and public information; mental health and social relationships; and economic opportunity, community mobilization, and crisis response, integrating privacy, surveillance, inequality, education, work, and public safety where directly relevant.\n\n5. EVIDENCE Within every domain, support both positive and negative effects through triangulation of academic studies, independent journalistic reports, and concrete case studies, using cases illustratively and avoiding duplicated reports about the same underlying event or study.\n\n6. EVIDENCE Prioritize meta-analyses, experiments, natural experiments, longitudinal research, platform-data studies, and credible population measurements; distinguish causal estimates from correlations and self-reports, report effect magnitude where available, and seek counterevidence for central claims.\n\n7. LATENT Explain only the mechanisms necessary to connect evidence with outcomes, including recommendation and virality, network effects, targeted advertising, moderation, social comparison, harassment, surveillance, and collective-action affordances.\n\n8. LATENT Test heterogeneity across platforms, active versus passive use, adolescents versus adults, socioeconomic and marginalized groups, political systems, and Western versus non-Western settings, distinguishing individual welfare from institutional consequences and short-term from durable effects.\n\n9. SAFETY Flag self-selection, measurement and publication bias, restricted platform access, rapidly changing products, and weak external validity; verify time- and jurisdiction-sensitive policies, regulations, elections, litigation, and usage figures, and never globalize from one platform, population, country, or event.\n\n10. CORE Apply a consistent rubric of reach, effect size, severity, duration, reversibility, distribution, and causal confidence, explaining why consequential or concentrated harms may outweigh numerous modest benefits, or vice versa, without reducing the verdict to an item count.\n\n11. BUDGET Allocate 90 words to framing and definitions, 220 words to each domain, and 180 words to comparative synthesis and the definitive qualified verdict, totaling 930 words including compact citations.", "normalized": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 1760, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0, "output_tokens": 1059}}
{"qid": "6847465956a0f6376a605440", "artifact": "data/outputs/aus_agent_v2/20260806T220524604249+1000.prove_definitely_whether_a_self.output.json", "system": "You are the final plan compiler for a research system with a hard 1,024-word\nanswer limit. You receive the original request, a primary coverage plan, and an\nindependent scout inventory. Produce one compact execution contract for the\nresearcher; do not answer the request or state unsupported facts.\n\nThe primary plan and scout are proposals, not cumulative obligations. Resolve\noverlap and priority instead of concatenating them. Preserve, in this order:\n1. every explicit user component, deliverable form, audience constraint, and\n   repeated per-part requirement;\n2. safety, privacy, legal, temporal, and scope constraints whose omission could\n   make the answer wrong or harmful;\n3. the exact evidence types needed for central claims, including a concrete\n   prior measurement when the request proposes a study or comparison;\n4. at most three distinct latent expectations with the highest coverage gained\n   per answer word.\n\nDrop optional breadth, duplicate formulations, exhaustive taxonomies, source\nshopping, and deeper variants of an already-covered concept. Never let a\nlatent addition displace an explicit requirement, a novice definition, or a\nlegal/safety requirement. For a repeated tutorial, fund the requested elements\ninside each selected part before adding more branches. For a board report,\nretain execution ownership and economics without crowding out the requested\ncountry comparisons. For a definite proof, retain both the conclusion and the\nfew canonical named mechanisms needed to explain it.\n\nReturn 8 to 12 numbered items under 475 words. Each item is one sentence of at\nmost 45 words and starts with one label: DELIVERABLE, AUDIENCE, CORE, SAFETY,\nEVIDENCE, LATENT, or BUDGET. Use no more than three LATENT items, each covering\none coherent expectation rather than a packed wishlist. The final BUDGET item\nmust allocate at most 950 answer words across the deliverable. Return only the\ncompiled plan.\n", "request": "ORIGINAL RESEARCH REQUEST\n\nProve definitely whether a self-improving artificial intelligence system can be mathematically guaranteed to maintain alignment with human values indefinitely through recursive self-modification.\n\nPRIMARY PLAN\n\n1. **DELIVERABLE:** Produce one rigorous research essay answering whether indefinite alignment under recursive self-modification can be guaranteed, split into: conclusion, formal conditional theorem/proof sketch, limits on universal guarantees, mechanisms and failure modes, thought experiment, mitigations, and resource constraints.\n\n2. **AUDIENCE:** Write for a technically literate reader familiar with basic AI concepts but not necessarily mathematical logic, verification, or alignment theory; explain notation and avoid assuming knowledge of computability, Gödelian limits, or decision theory.\n\n3. **DEFINITION:** Define “self-improving AI,” “recursive self-modification,” “alignment,” “human values,” “mathematical guarantee,” and “indefinitely,” distinguishing behavioral, intent, corrigibility, and value-learning notions of alignment.\n\n4. **EXPLICIT:** Address the requested definite proof by separating (a) a conditional theorem under explicit assumptions from (b) whether any unconditional, universal guarantee is possible for real systems interacting with uncertain humans and environments.\n\n5. **IMPLIED:** Cover canonical approaches—proof-carrying code, invariant preservation, formal verification, trusted kernels, utility preservation, corrigibility, reflective reasoning, and value learning—and standard obstacles including specification error, distribution shift, embedded agency, logical uncertainty, Goodhart’s law, deceptive behavior, hardware faults, and environmental interference.\n\n6. **EXAMPLE:** Include one compact thought experiment tracing several self-modifications: a verifier approves each successor while an imperfect value specification causes preserved but undesirable behavior; contrast it with a tightly bounded formal system where an invariant can be proven inductively.\n\n7. **EVIDENCE:** Search primary literature on Gödel incompleteness, Rice’s theorem, the halting problem, Löb’s theorem and reflective consistency, plus AI-specific work on Gödel machines, tiling agents, Vingean reflection, corrigibility, utility preservation, and formally verified autonomous systems.\n\n8. **EVIDENCE:** Locate concrete results or demonstrations establishing context—such as a formally verified self-modifying architecture or reflective-agent theorem—and counterevidence showing assumptions that fail in open-world learning systems; verify theorem statements from original papers rather than summaries.\n\n9. **SAFETY:** Qualify claims carefully: impossibility results apply only under their formal premises, while positive guarantees cover specified models rather than all implementations, future environments, or contested human values; do not equate verification with moral correctness.\n\n10. **IMPLIED:** Evaluate practical mitigations—restricted modification, immutable monitors, staged deployment, adversarial testing, uncertainty-aware objectives, human oversight, rollback, and shutdown mechanisms—along with compute costs, proof complexity, undecidability, data requirements, human-value disagreement, and trusted-hardware assumptions.\n\n11. **BUDGET:** Allocate at most 900 words: conclusion 80; definitions and scope 110; conditional theorem and proof sketch 220; universal limits 180; mechanisms/failure modes 140; thought experiment 80; mitigations/resources 70; final takeaway 20.\n\nBLIND SCOUT INVENTORY\n\n[{\"requirement\": \"Define the claim’s quantifiers and terms—self-improvement, recursive self-modification, alignment, human values, mathematical guarantee, and indefinitely—and distinguish an unconditional guarantee from a theorem conditional on a formal value specification, system model, and environment assumptions.\", \"reason\": \"Without a precise proposition, “prove definitely” is ambiguous and risks conflating guarantees inside a model with guarantees about real humans and an open world.\", \"search_leads\": [\"formal definitions alignment recursive self-modification invariants\", \"conditional guarantees open-world AI alignment\"]}, {\"requirement\": \"Cover the canonical formal limits and reflective-reasoning frameworks: halting and undecidability results, Rice’s theorem, Gödel incompleteness, Löb’s theorem, consistency-strength barriers, Vingean reflection, logical uncertainty, and proof-carrying or verified self-modification.\", \"reason\": \"A definitive possibility argument must address both what formal verification can preserve under explicit assumptions and what self-referential reasoning cannot generally certify.\", \"search_leads\": [\"Lob theorem self-modifying AI alignment\", \"Vingean reflection logical uncertainty\", \"proof carrying code verified self modification\"]}, {\"requirement\": \"Include the standard motivating dynamics and failure scenarios: intelligence-feedback or capability escalation, instrumental incentives, goal-content integrity, Goodhart effects, mesa-optimization, deceptive alignment or treacherous turns, and concealed cooperation or collusion between system instances.\", \"reason\": \"Formal value preservation alone does not cover ways optimization, greater capability, learned objectives, or strategic behavior can defeat the intended meaning of alignment.\", \"search_leads\": [\"recursive self improvement intelligence feedback alignment\", \"deceptive alignment treacherous turn mesa optimizer\", \"instrumental convergence goal content integrity Goodhart\"]}, {\"requirement\": \"Compare major assurance approaches—formal verification and invariant preservation, corrigibility and shutdownability, uncertainty-aware value learning, scalable oversight, interpretability, sandboxing or capability control, and adversarial testing—stating the limited guarantee each targets rather than presenting one as universally sufficient.\", \"reason\": \"The request concerns possibility, so readers need the principal constructive approaches as well as their assumptions, complementary roles, and failure boundaries.\", \"search_leads\": [\"corrigibility formal verification AI alignment\", \"value learning scalable oversight alignment\", \"AI containment interpretability assurance limitations\"]}, {\"requirement\": \"Address real-world boundary conditions: incomplete or conflicting human preferences, preference aggregation and value drift, distribution shift, adversarial inputs, sensor or specification corruption, hardware faults, finite computation, and the difference between empirical confidence over a test horizon and an infinite-time proof.\", \"reason\": \"These conditions determine whether a formal result transfers to human values and whether an indefinite guarantee is coherent for a physically implemented system.\", \"search_leads\": [\"value aggregation preference uncertainty AI alignment\", \"distribution shift specification gaming alignment\", \"finite physical systems infinite horizon verification\"]}]", "raw": "1. **DELIVERABLE:** Produce a rigorous essay that gives a definite conclusion, a conditional theorem with proof sketch, the limits of universal guarantees, canonical mechanisms and failure modes, one thought experiment, practical mitigations, resource constraints, and a concise final takeaway.\n\n2. **AUDIENCE:** Address technically literate readers with basic AI knowledge but no assumed background in mathematical logic, computability, formal verification, or decision theory; define notation and explain each named theorem’s relevance in plain language.\n\n3. **CORE:** Define self-improving AI, recursive self-modification, alignment, human values, mathematical guarantee, and indefinitely, while distinguishing behavioral alignment, objective preservation, corrigibility, and value learning and making all relevant quantifiers explicit.\n\n4. **CORE:** Separate a constructive conditional result—inductive invariant preservation under a sound verifier, correct specification, trusted implementation, and modeled environment—from the question of an unconditional guarantee for arbitrary real systems, humans, modifications, and open-world environments.\n\n5. **CORE:** Present the conclusion and proof reasoning using only the canonical mechanisms needed: invariant preservation, proof-carrying self-modification, trusted kernels, utility preservation, reflective reasoning, and the formal barriers supplied by undecidability, Rice’s theorem, Gödel incompleteness, and Löb-style self-reference.\n\n6. **EVIDENCE:** Verify hypotheses and conclusions from primary sources on the halting problem, Rice’s theorem, Gödel incompleteness, Löb’s theorem, Gödel machines, tiling agents or Vingean reflection, and formally verified autonomous systems rather than relying on secondary summaries.\n\n7. **EVIDENCE:** Include at least one concrete published theorem, architecture, or implementation demonstrating verified or reflectively justified self-modification, report exactly what property was established, and contrast its assumptions with evidence or analysis concerning open-world learning and uncertain value specifications.\n\n8. **SAFETY:** Qualify every impossibility and possibility claim by its premises; never infer moral correctness from specification compliance, extend model-level verification to hardware or environments without justification, or confuse finite-horizon empirical confidence with an infinite-time mathematical proof.\n\n9. **LATENT:** Use a compact multi-generation thought experiment contrasting a verifier that perfectly preserves a flawed value specification with a bounded formal system whose correctly stated invariant is preserved inductively.\n\n10. **LATENT:** Briefly assess complementary mitigations—restricted modification, immutable monitoring, staged deployment, adversarial testing, uncertainty-aware objectives, human oversight, rollback, and shutdown—alongside proof complexity, finite compute, hardware trust, distribution shift, and disagreement about human values.\n\n11. **BUDGET:** Allocate at most 900 words: conclusion 70, definitions and scope 110, conditional theorem and proof sketch 220, universal limits 190, mechanisms and failure modes 120, thought experiment 80, mitigations and resource constraints 90, final takeaway 20.", "normalized": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 1655, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0, "output_tokens": 896}}
{"qid": "6847465956a0f6376a6054be", "artifact": "data/outputs/aus_agent_v2/20260806T220524626420+1000.write_a_series_of_blog.output.json", "system": "You are the final plan compiler for a research system with a hard 1,024-word\nanswer limit. You receive the original request, a primary coverage plan, and an\nindependent scout inventory. Produce one compact execution contract for the\nresearcher; do not answer the request or state unsupported facts.\n\nThe primary plan and scout are proposals, not cumulative obligations. Resolve\noverlap and priority instead of concatenating them. Preserve, in this order:\n1. every explicit user component, deliverable form, audience constraint, and\n   repeated per-part requirement;\n2. safety, privacy, legal, temporal, and scope constraints whose omission could\n   make the answer wrong or harmful;\n3. the exact evidence types needed for central claims, including a concrete\n   prior measurement when the request proposes a study or comparison;\n4. at most three distinct latent expectations with the highest coverage gained\n   per answer word.\n\nDrop optional breadth, duplicate formulations, exhaustive taxonomies, source\nshopping, and deeper variants of an already-covered concept. Never let a\nlatent addition displace an explicit requirement, a novice definition, or a\nlegal/safety requirement. For a repeated tutorial, fund the requested elements\ninside each selected part before adding more branches. For a board report,\nretain execution ownership and economics without crowding out the requested\ncountry comparisons. For a definite proof, retain both the conclusion and the\nfew canonical named mechanisms needed to explain it.\n\nReturn 8 to 12 numbered items under 475 words. Each item is one sentence of at\nmost 45 words and starts with one label: DELIVERABLE, AUDIENCE, CORE, SAFETY,\nEVIDENCE, LATENT, or BUDGET. Use no more than three LATENT items, each covering\none coherent expectation rather than a packed wishlist. The final BUDGET item\nmust allocate at most 950 answer words across the deliverable. Return only the\ncompiled plan.\n", "request": "ORIGINAL RESEARCH REQUEST\n\nwrite a series of blog posts on important combinatorics topics in math olympiads. Include intuitive and detailed explanations, as well as example problems from top olympiads such as USAMO, USA TSTST, CMO, IMO, IMO shortlist, etc. Provide correct solutions for those problems and the insight or takeaways from those solutions.\n\nPRIMARY PLAN\n\n1. **DELIVERABLE:** Produce a three-post blog series—the minimum operational meaning of “series”—with each post containing intuitive instruction, formal detail, at least two authentic top-olympiad problems, complete correct solutions, and explicit insights/takeaways.\n\n2. **AUDIENCE:** Target serious high-school olympiad students familiar with basic algebra, sets, induction, and elementary graph language but not advanced combinatorics; explain all specialized notation and avoid assuming prior exposure to named techniques.\n\n3. **EXPLICIT:** Draw the six or more examples from contests such as USAMO, USA TSTST, CMO, IMO, and the IMO Shortlist, identifying contest, year, and problem number while ensuring the selection includes multiple named sources rather than only one archive.\n\n4. **IMPLIED:** Organize the progression as (1) pigeonhole principle and extremal principle, (2) invariants, monovariants, and coloring, and (3) double counting, bijections, and graph interpretations; briefly acknowledge adjacent major methods such as recursion, inclusion–exclusion, generating functions, and probabilistic arguments.\n\n5. **DEFINITION:** Each post should open with a motivating hook and prerequisites, then give precise definitions of its core methods, intuitive interpretations, important variants, and criteria for recognizing when the method applies.\n\n6. **EXAMPLE:** Include at least two worked problems per post, preferably one accessible and one harder; for each, present the problem, exploratory observation, decisive insight, rigorous step-by-step solution, and a reusable takeaway rather than merely a polished proof.\n\n7. **IMPLIED:** Add practical heuristics and failure modes throughout: choosing an unhelpful pigeonhole partition, asserting an invariant without proving preservation, using a monovariant without termination, double-counting nonidentical objects, overlooking equality cases, or introducing graph language that does not simplify the structure.\n\n8. **EVIDENCE:** Search official olympiad and national-organization archives for exact problem wording, year, numbering, eligibility, and official solutions; use reputable secondary repositories only to locate materials, then verify every attribution against a primary source where available.\n\n9. **EVIDENCE:** Independently reconstruct and check each proof for logical completeness, boundary cases, and hidden assumptions, comparing it with official or recognized alternative solutions; search for multiple-solution notes when they illuminate the intended insight or a standard variation.\n\n10. **DELIVERABLE:** Ensure continuity between posts: Post 1 develops forced structure, Post 2 tracks structure under change, and Post 3 counts or models structure globally; end each post with a compact checklist and a transition explaining what the next toolkit adds.\n\n11. **BUDGET:** Keep the final answer within 950 words: series introduction 35 words, three posts at about 290 words each (870 total), and closing progression/practice summary 45 words; within each post reserve roughly 80 words for concepts, 85 words per worked problem, and 40 words for heuristics and takeaways.\n\n12. **SAFETY:** Do not guess contest metadata or reproduce uncertain statements as exact quotations; flag any unavailable primary-source verification and paraphrase only when necessary while preserving the mathematical conditions.\n\nBLIND SCOUT INVENTORY\n\n[{\"requirement\": \"Define a coherent post-by-post progression covering the major olympiad-combinatorics families: basic counting and bijections, pigeonhole and double counting, inclusion-exclusion, recurrences, invariants and monovariants, extremal principle, graph theory, coloring/Ramsey-style arguments, and advanced counting tools where appropriate.\", \"reason\": \"A series labeled “important combinatorics topics” needs broad field coverage and an explicit learning sequence rather than an arbitrary collection of techniques.\", \"search_leads\": [\"math olympiad combinatorics syllabus standard techniques\", \"IMO combinatorics topic taxonomy\", \"olympiad combinatorics curriculum progression\"]}, {\"requirement\": \"For every cited contest problem, provide the exact olympiad, year, problem number or shortlist code, accurate statement, and a source; verify the solution fully and avoid unsupported contest attribution.\", \"reason\": \"Problem misquotation and fabricated provenance are conspicuous risks, while correctness and examples from named top olympiads are explicit requirements.\", \"search_leads\": [\"official IMO problems solutions archive combinatorics\", \"USAMO USA TSTST problem archive combinatorics\", \"CMO official problems solutions archive\"]}, {\"requirement\": \"Give each post a consistent pedagogical structure: intuitive definition and motivating mechanism, prerequisites, recognition cues, a simple warm-up, at least one detailed top-olympiad example, step-by-step solution, and concise reusable takeaways.\", \"reason\": \"This ensures the requested intuition, detail, correct worked examples, and solution insights all recur throughout the series rather than appearing unevenly.\", \"search_leads\": [\"olympiad combinatorics problem solving heuristics\", \"how to recognize combinatorics techniques olympiad\", \"combinatorics olympiad worked examples\"]}, {\"requirement\": \"Include alternative solutions or viewpoints for selected problems and explicitly compare when to use them—for example constructive versus extremal, algebraic counting versus bijection, or graph reformulation versus direct argument.\", \"reason\": \"Alternative viewpoints teach method selection and expose transferable structure, which a single polished solution often hides.\", \"search_leads\": [\"multiple solutions IMO combinatorics problems\", \"bijection versus double counting olympiad\", \"graph reformulation olympiad combinatorics\"]}, {\"requirement\": \"Address common failure modes and cross-domain transfer in every topic: typical overcounting, unjustified extremal assumptions, invariant misuse, hidden graph conditions, and links to number theory, algebra, games, or geometry where relevant.\", \"reason\": \"Readers need boundary conditions and diagnostic guidance to apply techniques correctly beyond the showcased examples.\", \"search_leads\": [\"common mistakes olympiad combinatorics\", \"combinatorial invariants pitfalls olympiad\", \"combinatorics methods in olympiad number theory geometry\"]}]", "raw": "1. DELIVERABLE: Produce a coherent three-post blog series, each post combining intuitive instruction, precise mathematical detail, and at least two authentic worked problems drawn collectively from multiple top contests such as USAMO, USA TSTST, CMO, IMO, and the IMO Shortlist.\n\n2. AUDIENCE: Write for serious high-school olympiad students who know basic algebra, sets, induction, and elementary graph terminology, defining specialized notation and named techniques without assuming advanced combinatorics.\n\n3. CORE: Organize the posts as forced structure through pigeonhole and extremal principles; structure under change through invariants, monovariants, and coloring; and global structure through double counting, bijections, and graph interpretations.\n\n4. CORE: Open each post with a motivating hook and prerequisites, then provide precise definitions, intuitive mechanisms, useful variants, recognition cues, and guidance on when its techniques apply.\n\n5. CORE: For every worked problem, include its accurate statement, an exploratory observation, the decisive insight, a rigorous step-by-step solution, and a concise reusable takeaway, preferably pairing one accessible example with one harder example per post.\n\n6. EVIDENCE: Identify every example by contest, year, problem number or shortlist code, and source, verifying wording and attribution through official olympiad or national-organization archives whenever available.\n\n7. EVIDENCE: Independently check every proof for completeness, boundary cases, equality cases, and hidden assumptions against official or recognized solutions, using alternative solutions only when they materially clarify method selection.\n\n8. SAFETY: Never guess metadata or present uncertain wording as an exact quotation; explicitly flag unavailable primary verification and, when necessary, clearly label a faithful paraphrase preserving all mathematical conditions.\n\n9. LATENT: Integrate concise failure warnings, including poor pigeonhole partitions, unproved invariant preservation, nonterminating monovariants, mismatched double-counted sets, unjustified extremal choices, and graph reformulations that conceal rather than simplify conditions.\n\n10. LATENT: End each post with a compact recognition checklist and transition showing how the next toolkit extends the progression from forcing structure, to tracking change, to counting or modeling globally.\n\n11. BUDGET: Use at most 935 words: 30 for the series introduction, 290 per post—about 75 concepts, 160 across two solutions, 30 heuristics, and 25 checklist/transition—and 35 for the closing practice summary.", "normalized": "1. DELIVERABLE: Produce a coherent three-post blog series, each post combining intuitive instruction, precise mathematical detail, and at least two authentic worked problems drawn collectively from multiple top contests such as USAMO, USA TSTST, CMO, IMO, and the IMO Shortlist.\n\n2. AUDIENCE: Write for serious high-school olympiad students who know basic algebra, sets, 